SIGBOVIK papers are almost always a delightEDIT: From their 2026 publication>Permission to make digital or hard copies of portions of this work for personal use is granted;
permission to make digital or hard copies of portions of this work for classroom use is also granted,
but seems ill-advisedreply

But their auto generated API clients don't have the invariant baked into them, which makes them very rough to use in say Rust.They'll have something like this:    MySchema:
      type: object
      properties:
        enum_member:   { enum: [foo, bar] }
        enum_member_2: { enum: [quz, qux] }
      if:   { properties: { enum_member: { const: bar } } }
      then: { required: [enum_member_2] }

Except, this is not defined in schemas, this is merely defined in prose around the documentation.So your Rust API is full of Option, and it gets really annoying when you're testing things like say Bedrock, which takes 10 minutes, and then fails because what you submitted 10 minutes before doesn't match their schema.I understand this can be fixed by reading the documentation in more detail, but it has gotten so verbose, and sometimes you just want to start something.reply
